<worksheet>
<title>Factoring by Grouping</title>
<objectives>
<p>
    In this section we will see how to:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Determine the greatest common factor of an expression.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor out the greatest common factor from an expression.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor an expression with an even number of terms by grouping.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </p>
</objectives>
<p>
    blah blah blah
</p>
<example>
    <title>Indentify a GCF</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            What is the GCF of the expression <m>30x^2 + 18x^2</m>?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Since the lowest power of each term is <m>x^2</m>, 
            that is the GCF of the variable part. The GCF of 30 and 18 is 6,
            since <m>30 = 6\cdot 5</m> and <m>18=6\ cdot 3</m>. So the GCF is 
            <m>6x^2</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
    
</example>

</worksheet>